WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:03.814
(speak in foreign language)

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
- Gentlemen, again,
congratulations on the film.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.070
It is so incredible and congratulations

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.440
on the SAG nominations.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.090
And really you guys are
getting so much praise for it.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.140
But have to ask you about

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.140
how did you guys kind
of reconcile the fact

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.880
that such an American film was nominated

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.010
for best foreign language
at the Golden Globes?

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:29.870
I mean, I'm sure you
appreciate to be honored

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.410
but it seems so strange
for such an American story.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.360
- Yeah, you know it's weird

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.940
because in the back of my mind like

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.670
when I think about language,
when I talk to my mom and dad

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.440
we don't always connect
using Korean or English.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.130
- [Isaac] We just had gestures
and facial expressions

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.430
because we have this
natural language barrier.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.614
So I've always felt like

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:56.670
there should be no distinction of language

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.450
but that we're all really
speaking human to each other.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.470
You know, even if we're not
speaking English to each other.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.710
So when this happened and
there's this delineation

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.710
with language, it feels like
a, like an external sort

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.760
of category for me, which
I don't always understand.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.980
And more, I just feel more sympathy

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.890
to the people who really
feel a lot of offense to it

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.150
and feel like, you know, once
again, we're being treated

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.360
as foreigners and all this
stuff that I totally understand.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.800
But in my view, the characters
in this film speak human.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.230
And that's what they're
doing to each other,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.130
with their faces.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.290
- [Isaac] You see it in
Ye-ri, you see it in Steven

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.345
and Alan.

00:01:36.345 --> 00:01:39.420
And that's what I hope for this film,

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.870
that everybody will watch it
and not think about language.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.720
They don't even know if
they're reading subtitles

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.920
because they get lost
in the humanity of it.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.900
- Well I mean, if
they're watching the film

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.270
I don't see how they can't.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.290
And I think that's spared out in me

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.480
in this film is still certified
fresh critics, audiences.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.320
I'm so glad that more folks
are gonna get a chance

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.153
to see it.

00:01:58.153 --> 00:02:00.410
But Alan, I'm gonna have to ask you.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.033
As people start seeing it, you know

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.140
you got to get to go to the SAGS now.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.170
Are you excited to maybe, you
know, do your like zoom party

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.890
are you getting your suits
ready for award season?

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:11.953
- Yes.

00:02:11.953 --> 00:02:13.400
(laughs)

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.380
- What did you think
when you got nominated?

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.995
Can you give me your reaction?

00:02:17.995 --> 00:02:19.412
- I'm like, "Yei"

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.067
- That was the exact dance.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.730
- Was that the exact dance?

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.563
I love that.

00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:30.680
And I also love that this
is a film, like you said

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.260
as folks get a chance to see it,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.490
it sorta continues I think a great trend

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:37.110
that, you know director Bong talked about

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.220
when he was doing the
awards tour for "Parasite."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.960
Is folks being able to sort of like

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.840
hurdle the one inch barrier
subtitles to open up

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.180
an entire world of cinema.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.320
And Steven, I just
wanna talk to you as far

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.410
as like the Korean influence here.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.823
I mean, you've worked with
some of these filmmakers.

00:02:53.823 --> 00:02:56.713
Which films or filmmakers are
you hoping folks will discover

00:02:56.713 --> 00:02:59.080
thanks to what you guys have done here

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.500
and films like "Parasite"?

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.050
- Oh man.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.810
You know, I just hope, I don't know.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.580
I think there's so many wonderful talents

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:08.990
kind of everywhere.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:13.990
I see so many new Chinese
films being really beautiful.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.630
Korean films there's I
think "House of Hummingbird"

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.267
is a really wonderful film.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.510
- [Steven] "The world between us"

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.490
is a really wonderful film too.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.360
Yeah. I mean, I hope it
just opens up a dialogue

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.310
of understanding that like
these, like director Bong said,

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.310
that these subtitles are just
literally tiny, tiny barriers

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.445
of examining the humanity
that all of us share.

00:03:39.445 --> 00:03:42.040
And so hopefully we're headed there.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.307
- Yeah. I really love it.

00:03:43.307 --> 00:03:45.440
And I love the fact that,
you know, we're able

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.560
to cover these films and
really get to talk about it.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.340
Because yeah, it just opens
up a whole different world

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.620
of cinema and it seems
like it'll never be ending

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.040
cause there's always different
types of stories to be told.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.463
But Isaac, I have to talk
to you about this one

00:03:58.463 --> 00:04:00.480
because this is an immigrant story.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.360
And one thing I remarked on it is it's one

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.380
with very little racism throughout it,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.000
which I think it was an incredible choice

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.160
because so often immigrant tales

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.580
are very much rooted in that.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.500
And I thought that was
such an interesting choice

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.350
to tell this story.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.400
And I wondered at what point
did you make that decision

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.100
or is that just how it played out?

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.223
- I made that decision early on.

00:04:23.103 --> 00:04:25.450
I'm glad that you liked that choice.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.380
Because for me, that was kind of a gamble.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.070
Like I knew that people would
be expecting a lot of racism,

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:33.903
to be honest.

00:04:33.903 --> 00:04:37.043
I thought with a story like
this, you often see that.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.580
- [Isaac] But first of all,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.150
that wasn't my experience growing up.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.170
Like, I did encounter racism.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.930
But most of the time my
existence was not defined

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:47.930
by that racism.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.840
And it wasn't defined by
that in my own family.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.190
Most of the time I'm just
trying to relate to my family,

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.350
to be honest.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.760
And then my interactions
with the community

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.520
there are so many that
just ended with love.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.540
Maybe it started with
friction, but it really ends

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.683
with people seeing each
other for who they are.

00:05:06.594 --> 00:05:09.020
So I wanted the film to
function in that way.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.690
I didn't want it to just knee jerk say,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.450
well these are white southerners

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.590
that they're gonna be racist.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.703
Like I think that in
itself is problematic.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.943
So yeah, I hope that resonates somehow.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.310
- I thought it was really incredible.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.790
And again, it really
allows you to just focus in

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.870
on the family and the dynamic.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:35.410
And one of the great dynamics
of course, is Steven and Alan

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.280
and the way you guys
had to do that together.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.670
So I wanna go back to the
whole audition process, Alan

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.640
because I do know that
you very much stood out.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.030
So do you remember that
day when you were working

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.490
with Steven and you knew
you were trying to audition

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:49.633
for it at all?

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.783
- Yeah. I remember some of it.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.287
We went to "Plan B."

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:03.193
I went into a room and that Steven the...

00:06:07.355 --> 00:06:10.023
- Casting director?
- Yeah.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.437
- Casting director and
Steven taught me like

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.357
something called (speaks
in foreign language).

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.340
- Oh, that's right.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.343
Oh, you got a good memory.

00:06:23.343 --> 00:06:25.530
- All right, Steven,
what's this real quick?

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.140
So I wanna hear all about it.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.002
- Oh it's like Korean cogs.

00:06:30.002 --> 00:06:32.353
- Oh, nice. Oh, that's dope.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:36.630
Oh, so do you like bang
them against each other?

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.040
- Yeah you try to flip the other person's

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.190
and you get to take it.

00:06:40.267 --> 00:06:42.590
But that's so keen that you remember that.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:43.960
I actually forgot about that.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.060
So you have a better memory than I do.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.440
Was I scary though, Alan?

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.510
Like when I came back into
the room as was your dad.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.584
- No, not really.
- Dang.

00:06:52.584 --> 00:06:54.747
- Alan's never scared.
- Never scared.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:56.090
- Never seen him scared.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:56.923
- I mean, and I...

00:06:56.923 --> 00:07:00.310
- People agreed that
Alan said Isaac was like,

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:01.220
hey you know that...

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.207
like after we did the
end props he was like

00:07:02.207 --> 00:07:05.374
"You know, that were,
this was pretend right?"

00:07:05.374 --> 00:07:06.770
We're done now.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.667
And then Alan was just
like, "Yeah, yeah. I know."

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:12.167
(crew laughs)

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.702
No in real life, I'm like "That was fake?"

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:19.147
(crew laughs)

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:21.480
- Was that when, you knew, Isaac

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.990
that he was the kid when he was
just like, "Yeah, I got it."

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:24.982
Was that the moment where you're like

00:07:24.982 --> 00:07:26.160
"All right, we're good."

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.126
- I mean, he convinced me, he fooled me.

00:07:28.126 --> 00:07:30.750
I thought, you know he was
really scared or something,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:31.730
but then it turns out you know,

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.810
he's just in the scene, he's acting.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.300
So when you see that, you know

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.200
that you're gonna be all right on set.

00:07:39.947 --> 00:07:43.697
(speaks in foreign language)

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:54.750
- First of all, congratulations
on the nominations.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.420
It's been incredible,
especially the SAG nominations.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.680
I'm just curious, what are you...

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:00.700
I'll start with you, Ye-ri,

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.380
what are you thinking
about the award season?

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:05.040
Are you getting excited
to, you know, come in

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.113
and sort of I guess, meet
the glitterati of Hollywood?

00:08:12.151 --> 00:08:15.901
(speaks in foreign language)

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:46.980
- Enjoy it. It's really incredible.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.190
And I think even more
people are gonna start

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.040
to appreciate it.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.103
But let's backtrack just a little bit.

00:08:54.103 --> 00:08:56.290
Yuh-Jang I'll start with you.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:58.500
This is one of the first big things

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.580
I think a lot of Americans
have seen you in.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.580
I, you know, spotted you in "Sense8",

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.360
but for other folks this may
be their first introduction.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.285
So I'm just curious.

00:09:07.285 --> 00:09:10.280
What made you sign on to do this one?

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.685
- Because of the friends.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:16.930
Now, you know, when you
get old, you can just focus

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.170
just the one thing.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.170
Not when you are young.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:23.850
But you can just, okay, this is script.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.660
- [Yuon] Script will be good for me.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.840
Good for my future or something like that.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:33.840
But now after 60 years old
I'm just focusing only people.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:35.390
The one I love.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:40.390
The one I appreciate or the
one who's very precious to me

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:45.890
that my precious friend
introduced the script.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.470
And then of course the script wasn't good,

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.043
then maybe I would say no.

00:09:51.912 --> 00:09:54.190
But while I was reading it

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.190
and then I felt very
genuine and authentic.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:01.410
So I said...

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:03.917
Middle of the reading I called her,

00:10:03.917 --> 00:10:06.040
"Is it real story of him?"

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.250
And she said, "Yes."

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:09.520
So I said, "Okay, I'll do it."

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.370
That's the start.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:12.673
- I love that.

00:10:12.673 --> 00:10:13.610
I love that so much

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.360
because it came from a
place from the heart.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.160
And you can tell that when
you see the performances

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:20.170
because they're so touching
and they're so moving.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.430
You can tell that everybody
became a family on set

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.170
and Ye-ri, Monica, especially
I find your performance of her

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:29.830
to be such a delicate thing.

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:33.580
Because she keeps her
emotions close to her chest

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.100
at the beginning and she
slowly unravels them.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:37.580
So I wondered if you could talk about

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:41.090
how you played that to
give it a slow reveal

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:42.423
of how she felt.

00:10:44.205 --> 00:10:47.955
(speaks in foreign language)

00:11:57.106 --> 00:11:58.120
That's so beautiful.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.110
And yeah, that's exactly it.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:01.920
Because when you hit that climax,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.850
you especially spiel in it.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.730
I mean, it really hits home
the whole emotion of the film.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.850
Before I get out of here, Yuh- Jung

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.320
I wanted to talk about Alan.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.040
Because I just got done interviewing him.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:15.330
And from the first time I met
him at like "Sundance" to now

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.340
he is just effervescent and just

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:21.570
like this ball of kid
energy, which is infectious

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:25.340
but see like a little
hurricane, it's hard to contain.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.380
So talk about containing
the hurricane to give

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.680
that beautiful performance
that you guys were able to do.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.790
Cause I'm sure there's a
lot of work on your side

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:35.660
to make that happen as well.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:36.810
- Sorry to disappoint you.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.780
I didn't work a lot with him.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.440
It's all Isaac's job

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.220
Because the director
knows how to control him.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.380
First I was scared to just
meet him because I heard

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.400
that he has not having
experienced to acting.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.400
- [Yuon] So I felt, "Oh my goodness

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.390
what I'm going to do with this boy?"

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:58.382
And then, Oh, no, no, no, no.

00:12:58.382 --> 00:13:02.003
He really made me disappointed.

00:13:03.425 --> 00:13:05.030
He memorized the older line,

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.920
and then Isaac asked him to do with me,

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.860
just the unit get over the line together.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.580
And then he was perfect
memorizing all the line.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:20.580
And then after that Isaac
asked him to do this.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:26.597
You need some kind of
shot I think from him

00:13:26.597 --> 00:13:28.940
just to smile, look at me and smile.

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:31.580
And this kind of smile or that smile

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:34.290
and then look at the camera

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.680
and then give you a Brown
face or something like that.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:41.331
It's all editing's job.
It's all Isaac's job.

00:13:41.331 --> 00:13:44.998
(speak in foreign language)

00:13:50.812 --> 00:13:52.895
(scream)

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:02.750
- [Jacqueline] I checked
this this morning.

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:03.650
I didn't know until today.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.010
You're on a five certified,
fresh, hot streak.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:07.990
Your last five films going back.

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:09.840
Yeah. Have all been certified fresh

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.880
which is quite a remarkable feed.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.550
And this is all, you
know, post "Walking Dead".

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:15.610
And I think...

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:17.520
- [Jacqueline] There must've
been a lot of offers after that

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.440
and you could have gone a
lot of different directions

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.860
but I think looking at the
films you did, everything

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.170
from "Okja" and "Mayhem"
and "Sorry to bother you."

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:25.677
And now "Minari" "Burning."

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.020
Those are specific choices.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.910
So talk about how you made
the choice to do those films

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:33.563
or how you wanted to work
with those filmmakers.

00:14:34.582 --> 00:14:39.582
- You know, it's strange
because you're asking me now

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:44.710
from a kind of a retrospective
lens and, you know,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:48.380
maybe at this point kind
of compiled like a story

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:52.760
to tell about what happened
and how made these choices.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:56.256
But in those moments, like, I don't feel,

00:14:56.256 --> 00:14:58.940
I don't know if I had agency like that.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:00.010
I probably did.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:03.070
I certainly said no to things that I felt

00:15:05.703 --> 00:15:07.630
like were just other versions`

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.343
of things that I've tried before.

00:15:11.425 --> 00:15:16.200
But I think in a lot of it
is, you know for me, I'm just,

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.680
I was really trying to find myself.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.740
I was really trying to find out who I was

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:24.340
and kind of get to play in roles

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:27.727
that allowed me the space
to express the fullness

00:15:27.727 --> 00:15:31.807
of not me per se, but of a character.

00:15:31.807 --> 00:15:35.490
You know, as an Asian-American actor,

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:40.160
prior to "Walking dead", if I
had any opportunities at all

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.580
it was really mostly to
service larger narratives

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:48.580
or kind of be kind of like a,
you know, like a plot point

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:51.860
by which to weave the
main character around it.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.680
So I wanted to be the main character

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.180
that gets to have the plot
points to weave around.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.520
And, you know, even with
"Mayhem" I'm so thankful to them

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:03.453
for giving me my first chance
at like a leading role.

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:07.700
- [Steven] It wasn't a big
budget and we started in Serbia.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:10.890
But we had some really wonderful
people with us, you know,

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.948
Samar was incredible. Joe was great.

00:16:13.948 --> 00:16:17.675
And, just to kind of do
that and flex that muscle

00:16:17.675 --> 00:16:18.850
was attractive to me.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:22.330
And then "Okja" I think was, you know,

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:24.300
who wouldn't work for a director Bong?

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:25.260
You know I'm a huge fan.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:29.230
And so, you know, him reaching
out and giving that part

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:32.170
to me was extraordinary and painful

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:36.860
because K was you know,
very much something

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:37.890
that I was at the time.

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:40.800
I was just somebody straddling two worlds

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.440
and being unable to service either one.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:47.210
And I think going through that experience

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:49.180
I wasn't necessarily super conscious of

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:51.050
what I was participating in.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.137
I would, but like I realized
halfway through, I was like

00:16:54.137 --> 00:16:56.910
"Oh man, like, this is
kind of how I feel."

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.752
And it is a painful realization.

00:16:59.752 --> 00:17:01.350
- [Steven] And then "Sorry to bother you"

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.700
was really wonderful of
just kind of speaking

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:05.630
from an Asian-Americans perspective.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:07.290
Even though it wasn't explicitly,

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.220
but just in a different way of...

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:11.590
- You're "Mr. Steal,
your girl" in that movie.

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:12.700
That's what I love.

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:14.710
Like she is, was the guy that was coming

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:16.850
to slot into the DMS to check on it.

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:18.793
I thought that was so brilliant.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.300
- You know, that's Boots, you know.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:25.050
And I guess on a larger note, what I feel

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.190
like is happening when
you think about, you know

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.063
Gong and then you think of an Isaac.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.290
I'm just really attracted to people

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:36.750
that are singular people, you know.

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.260
There's no other Boots Rileys.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.890
There are no other Bongs or
(mumbles) or Isaac Chungs.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.040
It's really just these people
that I gravitate towards

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:49.400
that we can share an experience in a story

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:51.540
that they've crafted that feels so unique.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.750
I remember reading, "Sorry to bother you"

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:56.810
and like certain people reading
and be like, "What is this?"

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.160
And then me reading, it'd be
like, "I gotta to do this."

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:00.440
Like I have to.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:02.390
Who can write like this?

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:04.130
Who has a mind like this?

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:06.403
And when I see that I'm in.

00:18:08.542 --> 00:18:10.592
So that's kind of where I'm at with that.

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.480
- I love that you put it
like that cause you're right.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.390
And I'm sure you just
go on from job to job,

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.140
trying to do interesting work but

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.510
when you can look back on
it, it really is incredible.

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:21.343
And that was so cool.

00:18:21.343 --> 00:18:22.970
And I was like, getting ready for this

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.730
to kinda chat about that one too,

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:28.451
because the New York times profile I read

00:18:28.451 --> 00:18:30.650
where you kinda talked about this

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:32.630
and the identity and the
choices that you made

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.690
I found it was just so
fascinating and interesting.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.380
Because the character
that you play in "Manari"

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:41.360
is just like, not about
multi-faceted at all.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:44.110
He is just like a man on a mission.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:47.640
One focus, one goal,
not listening to really

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:49.260
any of the noise on the outside.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.940
And I think that's way
more difficult I think

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:53.970
than trying to play a
multi-faceted character

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:56.620
in some ways to add that humanity.

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.400
So talk about that balancing act.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:02.692
- Yeah. Jacob was an interesting person

00:19:02.692 --> 00:19:05.370
to kind of climb into the mind of...

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:07.840
Not because I didn't
understand them at all

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.610
but because I think
you're kind of exploring

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:14.760
the pain of being alone.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:18.620
- [Steven] The pain of isolation
in him, at least for me.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:21.290
And I think that's where I
found a lot of his humanity.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:25.520
That he means well and he wants things

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.350
and he loves his family
and he desperately wants

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:29.710
to provide for them.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:32.220
But he doesn't know how
to separate his role

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:35.150
and his duty and his
function from the love

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:37.770
that he can also show his family.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:42.614
And oftentimes that function
and duty takes the role of love

00:19:42.614 --> 00:19:44.750
that he shows for his family

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:46.960
and his family doesn't
know how to deal with that

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:50.070
or accept that because
maybe it's not really

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.530
the best way to convey love.

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.450
To just play your part.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:56.630
And in that way, I think, you know

00:19:56.630 --> 00:20:00.050
Jacob from the outside
looking in might look

00:20:00.050 --> 00:20:03.070
and feel like a patriarch
that's dragging his family

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:05.150
through his own ego.

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:07.760
And ultimately it is to some degree.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:08.980
But from his perspective

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:12.620
he's also just trying his best
to provide for his family.

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:14.900
- [Steven] And he's
doing it in the only way

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:16.520
he's ever known how.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.480
And that's to give his body
and mind up to that struggle.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.880
And so I think in some
ways he feels deeply alone

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:25.330
and isolated from his own family

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:28.210
because they can't see what
he's providing for them

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.410
or that's what he feels
like they can't see.

00:20:31.464 --> 00:20:35.113
So yeah, that was kind
of the journey of Jacob.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:37.750
- And it's interesting too, obviously

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.090
because this is semi-autobiographical

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:41.970
and I think even Isaac
would be able to say

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:44.200
that some of the stuff that
he experienced was stranger

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:45.900
than the fiction that he put on screen

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.470
but in the end playing his dad

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:50.640
and that's another delicate dance too.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:51.473
So I was just wondering

00:20:51.473 --> 00:20:53.210
if you guys had any
conversations with that.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.290
I'm sure he had to give
you a bit of freedom

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.160
with it a little bit, but
also he wanted to make sure

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.880
that it was true to the man
that he was trying to be.

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:02.980
- Well, you know, to be quite honest

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:04.870
he actually left a lot of space.

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:06.490
He left a lot of space.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:10.630
He had no specifics on
how his dad needed to be.

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:12.410
And in fact, he never brought that up

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:15.253
as something that he
needed to be checked off.

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:19.390
You know, he wrote something
so true in my opinion

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:23.680
that was so human that
I think it created space

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:27.280
for a lot of people to have
come in and taken that role

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:29.560
and imbued it with the
things that they knew.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.510
It just happened to be
that, you know, Isaac

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.740
and I share a lot of
our experiences together

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:39.740
of an immigrant life of isolation
and of also understanding

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:42.560
you know, what it means to be a father.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.400
- [Steven] And so, yeah, I think that

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:47.370
I think that really speaks
to him on a larger note

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.290
the graciousness of someone
like Isaac, you know.

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:55.290
As I get to know him more and
as I get to see how he thinks

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:01.360
and how he's constructed this
film and written this film

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:03.850
I peel back all those layers and I realize

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:05.350
what's at the beating heart of it all.

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:08.450
And it's just someone that
like deeply deeply cares

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:12.280
about others and about
humanity in that way.

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:16.020
And he cares not only about the
characters that he's created

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:18.940
but also he cares deeply
about even the audience.

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:22.130
About making sure that there is no barrier

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.760
to accessing these people in this story.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.270
And it's really about connection

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:28.420
- I guess you'd have to be. Yeah.

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:30.170
Cause you wouldn't wanna
be stifled in that.

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:32.600
It's really incredible too
cause it feels so lifted.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:34.300
It's even more incredible to realize

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:36.460
that it was just a creation
that you guys were able to do

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.331
from taking it from
the page to the screen.

00:22:39.331 --> 00:22:42.790
I guess I have to definitely
ask you about this one

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:45.550
in the fact that folks
are now gonna finally

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:47.960
get to see it and the scene.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.980
I guess it were that
you ended up with Ye-ri

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:50.880
towards the end.

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.330
It's probably one of the
most climactic things,

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:54.993
but without spoiling it,

00:22:54.993 --> 00:22:57.140
just talk about her as a scene partner

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.780
and how you guys were
able to work that out.

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:01.830
Because in a lot of ways,
the movie lives or dies

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:02.663
by that scene.

00:23:02.663 --> 00:23:05.877
- Wow, thanks. I'm so
glad you enjoyed that.

00:23:05.877 --> 00:23:09.321
You know, I can't say enough
good things about Ye-ri.

00:23:09.321 --> 00:23:10.743
She's incredible.

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:17.590
- [Steven] Her role in this
it's so meta too, you know.

00:23:21.958 --> 00:23:22.791
That particular scene...

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:26.760
I guess I gotta start from
how we shot the whole thing.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:30.040
You know, we never
really over talked much.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:32.960
We maybe talked about that
particular scene a lot

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:37.960
in terms of shaping what I
wanted to say in that moment

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:40.970
and maybe what she wanted
to say in that moment.

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:44.667
And we got to some place that
felt right for the two of us.

00:23:44.667 --> 00:23:47.760
- [Steven] But beyond that,
you know, there was many times

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.860
where her and I really
just had single takes.

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:53.060
We kind of really lived that experience.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:56.420
And the ways in which she
was honest about her approach

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:00.580
to Monica, in the ways that I
would talk about my approach

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.730
to Jacob and kind of the
truthfulness that we shared

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:07.330
between each other and
even in the disagreements

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:10.860
of how we saw situations
were part of it, you know.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.550
It was part of making the
tension of those characters.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.384
And so, yeah, I mean

00:24:16.384 --> 00:24:20.240
I knew I could just trust her
intrinsically to be honest

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:22.980
and truthful all the time.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:25.863
And you know, if you haven't
seen partner like that,

00:24:25.863 --> 00:24:28.875
like that's, you're chilling.

00:24:28.875 --> 00:24:30.355
(laughs)

00:24:30.355 --> 00:24:32.420
- I mean, it didn't look
like y'all were chilling,

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:33.510
it looked pretty powerful

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:34.720
but I get what you're saying as far

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.428
as the work on the other side.

00:24:36.428 --> 00:24:38.580
Last one and this one's a little bit light

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.810
but I'm just gonna put it out there.

00:24:40.810 --> 00:24:43.004
Your character, Jacob is
like the Michael Jordan

00:24:43.004 --> 00:24:44.733
of chicken sexting.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.300
We are very curious if
you got good at this.

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:49.930
And trust me when I say this was a very

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:52.530
big editorial conversation
at "Rotten Tomatoes."

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:54.070
So please take this seriously.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:57.010
We wanna know, did you get
good at telling the difference

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:58.037
between male and female?

00:24:58.037 --> 00:24:59.490
- I did, I got up pretty decent.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.373
You know actually who
probably got better is Ye-ri.

00:25:03.248 --> 00:25:05.160
Yeti is the real...

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:08.750
I'm probably like the Scottie Pippin.

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:12.330
No, maybe I'll get all the credit

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.650
because some dumb weirdness,

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:18.739
but she's the real MVP, you know?

00:25:18.739 --> 00:25:20.081
(mumbles) the real MVP.

00:25:20.081 --> 00:25:23.400
She really knew how to do that.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:27.430
I think for us, it was the
practice of practicing that

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:30.330
with our animal Wrangler
and also, you know, my wife

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:34.230
her parents also chicken sexed,
just like Isaac's parents.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:35.780
And they taught us tips and tricks

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:39.070
and we all grow out our
thumbnails so that we could like

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:40.143
open their vents.

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:44.340
It was a fascinating time.

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.510
- Oh my gosh, I really did not expect that

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:47.343
to be such a thing.

00:25:47.343 --> 00:25:48.176
So I will have to...

00:25:48.176 --> 00:25:49.009
I literally was like

00:25:49.009 --> 00:25:52.040
"He is not gonna say anything
about this chicken sex stuff,

00:25:52.040 --> 00:25:52.873
y'all are morbid."

00:25:52.873 --> 00:25:56.150
But you know what, I'm
down with that one now.

00:25:56.150 --> 00:25:59.060
And then just like a
very last question again

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.050
just to follow up on the "Parasite."

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:02.936
Now you guys entering in this conversation

00:26:02.936 --> 00:26:04.980
logical best picture nominee,

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:07.110
folks in my seat who do this for a living

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:09.530
are already talking about that thing.

00:26:09.530 --> 00:26:12.390
Man what do you think is going
to be like this next section

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:13.710
when we get back into theaters?

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:15.440
Do you think it's just
gonna push even further?

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:17.850
And if so, what are you
excited to see next?

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:19.070
What kinds of stuff would you like

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:23.780
to see American audiences
come sort of import over?

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:24.613
- I don't know.

00:26:24.613 --> 00:26:25.550
I mean, I think we're also...

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.870
I think for America,
what's fascinating about,

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:32.040
especially this film is
kind of really digging into

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:34.480
what's already been here, you know.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:35.990
And I think that's kind of touching

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:39.062
on what we were facing
with "Minari" in terms of,

00:26:39.062 --> 00:26:42.420
I think this conversation
around foreign language.

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:44.470
And even "Parasite."

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:48.240
Parasite opened many doors and
broke down a lot of barriers

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:50.910
on a larger, I think humanity level

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:53.653
of just understanding each
other in between cultures.

00:26:55.010 --> 00:27:00.010
But their humanity that they're
expressing is unencumbered

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:04.910
by the American gays because
they're Korean people

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:06.430
and they're making it from Korea.

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:09.070
So they're not considering us.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:12.490
Whereas our film does have to navigate

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:17.490
the constant consideration
of who we are in the midst of

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:21.200
a primarily white majority
American audience.

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:24.960
And so a lot of the time the
ways that that would express

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:27.300
is we end up explaining ourselves

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:32.300
or we end up telling the
audience how we are oppressed

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:37.660
by the majority or how the
majority factors into our story.

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:41.410
But the truth is we weren't
even considering that.

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:43.270
Because that's our story.

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:46.670
Our story doesn't bring
home a consideration

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:49.950
of the majority American
whiteness at a constant level.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:54.170
It's just like, I'm me and I exist.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.200
And this is from my point of view.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.710
And I think it was really
cool to be at a time

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:03.710
where an American company in
"Plan B" and A24 can finance

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:09.550
and produce and make a non
English language, American film.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:16.810
I mean, what a new foundation,
what a new frontier.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:20.420
And I hope that, you know

00:28:20.420 --> 00:28:22.700
if anything comes about it's that we get

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:27.700
to really understand
what makes America more.

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:29.500
All the things that have always been here,

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:31.210
which is just all of us.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.830
There's so many different
things that make America.

00:28:33.830 --> 00:28:35.900
And I'm interested in that

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.670
as a next step for us on American side.

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.440
And then beyond that the
world is just getting more

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:42.640
and more global anyhow.

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:44.260
And so, you know

00:28:44.260 --> 00:28:47.583
hopefully we get to be human
together at some point.

00:28:48.927 --> 00:28:51.510
(upbeat music)

